INSERT INTO `test_details` (`test_id`, `patient_id`, `doctor_id`, `doctor_approval`, `specimen`, `no_of_specimen`, `test_date`, `admin_archive`, `doctor_archive`, `hospital_archive`, `amount`, `pathologists_fee`, `specimen_fee`, `status`, `test_type_id`, `urgent`, `snomed_code`, `box_received`, `notes`, `Client_case_number`, `visiopath_number`, `hospital_id`, `clinical_details`, `specimen_text`, `macroscopic_details`) VALUES
(1, 1, 1, 1, '5', 10, '2020-01-13', 0, 0, 1, NULL, '150', '170', 'Unassigned', '5', 0, 'M1', 1, 'In the Quantum Measurement Problem the issue of what constitutes macroscopic and what constitutes the quantum world is unresolved and possibly unsolvable. The related Correspondence Principle can be articulated thus: every macroscopic phenomena can be formulated as a problem in quantum theory. A violation of the Correspondence Principle would thus ensure an empirical distinction between the macroscopic and the quantum. ', 'crn20/1', '20VP1', 1, 'When applied to physical phenomena and bodies, the macroscopic scale describes things as a person can directly perceive them, without the aid of magnifying devices. This is in contrast to observations (microscopy) or theories (microphysics, statistical physics) of objects of geometric lengths smaller than perhaps some hundreds of micrometers.  A macroscopic view of a ball is just that: a ball. A microscopic view could reveal a thick round skin seemingly composed entirely of puckered cracks and fissures (as viewed through a microscope) or, further down in scale, a collection of molecules in a roughly spherical shape. An example of a physical theory that takes a deliberately macroscopic viewpoint is thermodynamics. An example of a topic that extends from macroscopic to microscopic viewpoints is histologyy. ', '2 Blood tests help doctors check for certain diseases and conditions. ... Specifically, blood tests can help doctors: Evaluate how well organs—such as the kidneys, liver, thyroid, and heart—are working. Diagnose diseases and conditions such as cancer, HIV/AIDS, diabetes, anemia (uh-NEE-me-eh), and coronary heart disease.', 'Colon, rectum, anus: Panproctocolectomy specimen measuring 25 mm in diameter x 40 mm in length across the small bowel and between 18 and 50 mm in diameter x 750 mm in length across the large bowel. No appendix. Three tumours seen, the first just distal to the ileocaecal valve, the second in the transverse colon and the third in the distal rectum.\n\nFirst tumour: There is a circumferential tumour distal to the ileocaecal valve measuring 3 x 30 x 55 mm involving muscularis propria. Tumour lies 5 mm from the serosal surface (no tumour perforation), 80 mm from the proximal resection margin and 25 mm from the circumferential resection margin. There is an adjacent distal sessile polyp measuring up to 30 mm, lying 30 mm, from the tumour.\n\nSecond tumour: There is a circumferential tumour in the transverse colon measuring 12 x 34 x 60 mm extending into the serosal fat. Tumour lies 4 mm from the serosal surface (no tumour perforation), 180 mm from the proximal resection margin and 20 mm from the circumferential resection margin.\n\nThird tumour: There is a polypoid tumour in the distal rectum below the anterior peritoneal reflection measuring 4 x 20 x 25 mm that focally involves muscularis propria. Tumour lies 15 mm from the serosal surface (no tumour perforation), 50 mm from the distal resection margin (anus) and 25 mm from the circumferential resection margin. There are multiple adjacent sessile polypoid lesions measuring up to 5 mm.\n\nBlocks: 1 = resection margins, 2 = apical lymph node (first and second tumour), 3 = subapical node, 4 = node 3, 5-11 = first tumour, 12 and 13 = polyp, 14-19 = second tumour, 20-23 = third tumour, 24-27 polypoid lesions, 28 = one node, 29 = 3 nodes, 30 = one node, 31 = apical lymph node (third tumour), 32 = subapical node, 33-55 fat.\n'),
(2, 2, 1, 1, '6', 1, '2020-01-16', 0, 1, 0, NULL, '120', '150', 'Unassigned', '6', 1, 'M2', 1, '', '1314/20', '20VP2', 1, 'When applied to physical phenomena and bodies, the macroscopic scale describes things as a person can directly perceive them, without the aid of magnifying devices. This is in contrast to observations (microscopy) or theories (microphysics, statistical physics) of objects of geometric lengths smaller than perhaps some hundreds of micrometers.  A macroscopic view of a ball is just that: a ball. A microscopic view could reveal a thick round skin seemingly composed entirely of puckered cracks and fissures (as viewed through a microscope) or, further down in scale, a collection of molecules in a roughly spherical shape. An example of a physical theory that takes a deliberately macroscopic viewpoint is thermodynamics. An example of a topic that extends from macroscopic to microscopic viewpoints is histologyy. ', 'Colon, rectum, anus: Panproctocolectomy specimen measuring 25 mm in diameter x 40 mm in length across the small bowel and between 18 and 50 mm in diameter x 750 mm in length across the large bowel. No appendix. Three tumours seen, the first just distal to the ileocaecal valve, the second in the transverse colon and the third in the distal rectum.\r\n\r\nFirst tumour: There is a circumferential tumour distal to the ileocaecal valve measuring 3 x 30 x 55 mm involving muscularis propria. Tumour lies 5 mm from the serosal surface (no tumour perforation), 80 mm from the proximal resection margin and 25 mm from the circumferential resection margin. There is an adjacent distal sessile polyp measuring up to 30 mm, lying 30 mm, from the tumour.\r\n\r\nSecond tumour: There is a circumferential tumour in the transverse colon measuring 12 x 34 x 60 mm extending into the serosal fat. Tumour lies 4 mm from the serosal surface (no tumour perforation), 180 mm from the proximal resection margin and 20 mm from the circumferential resection margin.\r\n\r\nThird tumour: There is a polypoid tumour in the distal rectum below the anterior peritoneal reflection measuring 4 x 20 x 25 mm that focally involves muscularis propria. Tumour lies 15 mm from the serosal surface (no tumour perforation), 50 mm from the distal resection margin (anus) and 25 mm from the circumferential resection margin. There are multiple adjacent sessile polypoid lesions measuring up to 5 mm.\r\n\r\nBlocks: 1 = resection margins, 2 = apical lymph node (first and second tumour), 3 = subapical node, 4 = node 3, 5-11 = first tumour, 12 and 13 = polyp, 14-19 = second tumour, 20-23 = third tumour, 24-27 polypoid lesions, 28 = one node, 29 = 3 nodes, 30 = one node, 31 = apical lymph node (third tumour), 32 = subapical node, 33-55 fat.\r\n', 'Colon, rectum, anus: Panproctocolectomy specimen measuring 25 mm in diameter x 40 mm in length across the small bowel and between 18 and 50 mm in diameter x 750 mm in length across the large bowel. No appendix. Three tumours seen, the first just distal to the ileocaecal valve, the second in the transverse colon and the third in the distal rectum.\r\n\r\nFirst tumour: There is a circumferential tumour distal to the ileocaecal valve measuring 3 x 30 x 55 mm involving muscularis propria. Tumour lies 5 mm from the serosal surface (no tumour perforation), 80 mm from the proximal resection margin and 25 mm from the circumferential resection margin. There is an adjacent distal sessile polyp measuring up to 30 mm, lying 30 mm, from the tumour.\r\n\r\nSecond tumour: There is a circumferential tumour in the transverse colon measuring 12 x 34 x 60 mm extending into the serosal fat. Tumour lies 4 mm from the serosal surface (no tumour perforation), 180 mm from the proximal resection margin and 20 mm from the circumferential resection margin.\r\n\r\nThird tumour: There is a polypoid tumour in the distal rectum below the anterior peritoneal reflection measuring 4 x 20 x 25 mm that focally involves muscularis propria. Tumour lies 15 mm from the serosal surface (no tumour perforation), 50 mm from the distal resection margin (anus) and 25 mm from the circumferential resection margin. There are multiple adjacent sessile polypoid lesions measuring up to 5 mm.\r\n\r\nBlocks: 1 = resection margins, 2 = apical lymph node (first and second tumour), 3 = subapical node, 4 = node 3, 5-11 = first tumour, 12 and 13 = polyp, 14-19 = second tumour, 20-23 = third tumour, 24-27 polypoid lesions, 28 = one node, 29 = 3 nodes, 30 = one node, 31 = apical lymph node (third tumour), 32 = subapical node, 33-55 fat.\r\n'),
(3, 3, 3, 1, '5', 1, '2020-01-17', 0, 0, 0, NULL, '20', '40', 'Unassigned', '5', 0, 'M3', 1, 'Please send copy to Dr Martin', '20H080098', '20VP3', 1, '+ve TTG, abdo pain, diarrhoea ? coeliac disease', 'Duodenal biopsy', 'Duodenal biopsy- 2 fragments 3mm each'),
(4, 4, 3, 1, '3', 3, '2020-01-21', 0, 0, 0, NULL, '20', '50', 'Unassigned', '3', 0, 'M4', 0, '', '20H000345', '20VP4', 1, 'Breast cancer, firm mass in left axilla, lymph node biopsy', 'A. Lymph node biopsy\nB. Firm mass left axilla\nC. Wide local excision left breast\nD. Sentinel node', 'A. Lymph node biopsy- A fatty lymph node 23 x 14 x 5mm\nB. Firm mass left axilla- A piece of fibro-fatty tissue 45 x 16 x 21mm. Slicing shows a firm cut surface\nC. Wide local excision- Specimen radiograph seen- No\nNeo-adjuvant therapy- No\nSpecimen type- Wide local excision (wire guided)      \nGuide wire present- Yes, one\nSpecimen laterality- Left\nSpecimen weight- 49g\nFibro-fatty tissue 23mm medial to lateral, 16mm superior to inferior, and 12mm superficial to deep\nOrientated- Yes, on orientation board\nInked as follows: \nSuperficial= red, deep= green, superior= orange, inferior= yellow, medial= blue, lateral= black.   \nMacroscopic abnormality noted- A white tumour is seen 8mm close to the deep margin\nThe specimen has been sliced from medial to lateral into 8 slices with slice 1 being the most medial.\n\nBlock Key:\n1- tumour      \n2- tumour to margin      \n3- tumour      \n4- slice 1\n5- slice 2\n6- slice 3\n7- slice 4\n8- slice 5\n'),
